  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "\"Life is {precious}. ... To a person nothing is more {precious} than their life, and if they {entrust} me with that life, how could I refuse that trust, saying I'm cold, hungry, or tired?\" These words of Dr Lin Qiaozhi give us a look into the heart of this amazing woman, and what carried her through a life of hard choices.",
      "cn": "\"生命是宝贵的。……对一个人来说，没有什么比生命更宝贵；而如果他们把生命托付给我，我怎么能以自己又冷、又饿、又累为由，拒绝这份信任呢？\"林巧稚医生的这番话让我们得以窥见这位非凡女性的内心，以及是什么支撑她度过了充满艰难抉择的一生。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "As a five-year-old girl, Lin Qiaozhi was deeply affected by her mother's death. At age 18, instead of following the traditional path of {marriage} like the {majority} of girls, she chose to study medicine. \"Why should girls learn so much? Finding a good husband should be their final goal!\" her brother complained, thinking of the high {tuition} fees. She responded, \"I'd rather stay single to study all my life!\"",
      "cn": "林巧稚五岁时，母亲的去世对她影响极深。十八岁时，她没有像大多数女孩那样走传统的婚姻道路，而是选择了学医。\"女孩子为什么要学那么多？找个好丈夫才是她们的最终归宿！\"想到高昂的学费，她的兄弟抱怨道。她回答说：\"我宁可一辈子单身，也要读书求学！\""
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "Eight years later, Lin graduated from Peking {Union} Medical College (PUMC) with the Wenhai {Scholarship}, the highest prize given to graduates. She immediately became the first woman ever to be hired as a resident {physician} in the OB-GYN department of the PUMC Hospital. Within six months, she was named a chief resident {physician}, a position that usually took four years to achieve. After working for a few years, she was sent to study in Europe and then, in 1939, in the US. She greatly impressed her American colleagues, who invited her to stay. Dr Lin, however, rejected the offer. She wanted to serve the women and children at home.",
      "cn": "八年后，林巧稚以\"文海\"奖学金从北京协和医学院毕业，该奖学金是授予毕业生的最高奖项。她随即成为北京协和医院妇产科聘用的第一位女住院医师。不到六个月，她便被任命为住院总医师，而这一职位通常需要四年才能晋升到。工作了几年之后，她被派往欧洲学习，随后于1939年又赴美学习。她给美国同行留下了深刻印象，他们邀请她留下。然而，林医生拒绝了邀请。她想为祖国的妇女儿童服务。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "In 1941, Dr Lin became the first Chinese woman ever to be appointed director of the OB-GYN department of the PUMC Hospital, but just a few months later, the department was closed because of the war. Thinking of all the people still in need of help, Dr Lin opened a private {clinic}. She charged very low fees to treat patients and often reduced costs for poor patients. At times she was even seen riding a donkey to {faraway} villages to provide medical care.",
      "cn": "1941年，林医生成为北京协和医院妇产科的首位中国女性主任，但仅仅几个月后，该科室就因战争关闭了。想到仍有许多人需要帮助，林医生开了一家私人诊所。她向病人收取的费用很低，并且常常为贫困病人减免费用。有时人们甚至能看到她骑着毛驴去偏远的村庄给人看病。"
    },
    {
      "num": "05",
      "en": "The new People's Republic of China saw Dr Lin Qiaozhi playing a key role. In 1954, she was elected to the first National People's Congress and, over the next several decades, she held many important positions. Her heart, however, was {elsewhere}. She was more interested in tending patients, publishing medical research on care for women and children, and training the next generation of doctors. \"The OB-GYN department cares for two lives,\" She told new {staff} in her department. \"As doctors, we should be responsible for the patients and treat them as our sisters.\"",
      "cn": "新中国成立后，林巧稚医生发挥了重要作用。1954年，她当选第一届全国人民代表大会代表，并在随后的几十年里担任了许多重要职务。然而，她的心却在别处。她更感兴趣的是照料病人、发表有关妇幼护理的医学研究，以及培养下一代医生。\"妇产科守护的是两条生命，\"她对科室的新员工说，\"作为医生，我们应该对病人负责，把她们当作自己的姐妹。\""
    },
    {
      "num": "06",
      "en": "Though Lin Qiaozhi never married, she was known as the \"mother of ten thousand babies\", having delivered over 50,000 babies in her lifetime. Dr Lin did not {retire} until the day she died, 22 April 1983. Since she had no children of her own, she left her savings to a {kindergarten} and a fund for new doctors. And even as she lay dying, her final thoughts were for others: \"I'm ready to go,\" she said. \"Don't try to rescue me any more. Don't waste the medicine any more.\"",
      "cn": "虽然林巧稚终身未嫁，但她被称为\"万婴之母\"，一生中接生了超过五万名婴儿。林医生工作到生命的最后一刻，直到1983年4月22日去世那天才退休。由于她自己没有孩子，她把积蓄留给了幼儿园和一项培养新医生的基金。即便在弥留之际，她最后的牵挂仍是他人：\"我准备走了，\"她说，\"不要再抢救我了，不要再浪费药品了。\""
    }
  ],
